# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Signal — Competitive Intelligence Agent
# Copy to `.env` and fill in real values. `.env` is gitignored.
# env.mjs uses first-value-wins, so ordering matters if a key appears twice.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ─── REQUIRED ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# OpenRouter API key — one key, swap models with a string.
# Sign up at https://openrouter.ai/. Fund the account OR add a BYOK Anthropic
# key at https://openrouter.ai/settings/integrations to bill Anthropic directly.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-v1-...

# Turso (libSQL) database — canonical signal store.
# Get free hosted DB at https://turso.tech/ (or `stripe projects add turso`).
# After signup: `turso db create signals` → `turso db show signals --url` for URL,
# `turso db tokens create signals` for the auth token.
# ── Database ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LOCAL (default for the demo — no account, no signup, no network):
#   the whole stack runs against a libSQL file. `npm run db:migrate` creates it.
TURSO_DATABASE_URL=file:./data/signals.db
TURSO_AUTH_TOKEN=

# HOSTED (for a real deployment — each fork/deployment gets its OWN database
# and keys; nothing here is shared, and this file is gitignored):
#   TURSO_DATABASE_URL=libsql://<your-db>.turso.io
#   TURSO_AUTH_TOKEN=<token>




# ─── RECOMMENDED (enables richer discovery) ──────────────────────────────────

# Tavily Search API — mention discovery beyond RSS feeds. Free tier = 1000
# credits/month. `npm run watch:tavily` uses 3 credits × (# competitors) per run.
# At 3 competitors × daily cadence that's ~270/month. Sign up at https://tavily.com/.
# Missing key is NOT an error — the watcher silently skips so `npm run all` still completes.
TAVILY_API_KEY=tvly-...

# Optional: cap monthly Tavily spend. Watcher refuses to run once this threshold
# is reached (auto-resets on the 1st of each month). Default: 800. Use --force to override.
# CI_TAVILY_MONTHLY_BUDGET=800

# Optional: minimum hours between Tavily runs. Default: 12 (so `npm run all`
# twice a day only spends Tavily credits once). Use --force to override.
# CI_TAVILY_MIN_HOURS_BETWEEN_RUNS=12


# ─── OPTIONAL: model overrides ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Any OpenRouter model slug works here. Check https://openrouter.ai/models.
# Defaults below match what openrouter.mjs ships with. Uncomment to override.
#
# Three knobs, each tied to a workload profile:
#
#   CI_CLASSIFIER_MODEL  — fires on every RSS / HN / Tavily result, thousands of
#                          calls per run. Optimize for $ per call. Needs to
#                          return reliable JSON (OpenRouter response_format).
#   CI_SYNTHESIS_MODEL   — battlecards + self-card. Dozens of calls per week.
#                          Needs solid structured-output instruction following.
#   CI_DEEP_MODEL        — analyst /deep /gap /outside /brief + `npm run research`.
#                          One call at a time; reasoning depth > everything.
#
# TWO TRAPS, BOTH MEASURED ON THIS DEPLOYMENT 2026-09-25 — read before you swap.
#
# 1. DO NOT PUT A REASONING MODEL ON THE CLASSIFIER. With `deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro`:
#    4,043 tokens in, **1,905 tokens out, 41.7 seconds** — per signal — to answer a
#    fixed-schema question like "is this a product launch". 146 signals took 101 minutes.
#    A reasoning model bills its deliberation as OUTPUT, the expensive side of every rate
#    card. Triage wants a fast instruct model, not a thinker. Symptom to watch:
#    `avg_s` in `npm run cost -- --by=model` in the tens of seconds.
#
# 2. "FLASH" DOES NOT MEAN CHEAP. On this workload `google/gemini-3.6-flash`
#    ($0.75/$3.75) costs MORE than `deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro` ($0.652/$1.304), because
#    output rate dominates. Compare the OUTPUT column first, always.
#
# Prices below are OpenRouter's own, per 1M input/output, checked 2026-09-25. They move —
# `npm run cost:estimate` prices your next run from your ledger instead of from this file.

# ── Classifier — per-signal triage. Highest volume, most cost-sensitive, least
#    quality-sensitive (it emits a fixed JSON verdict, not prose).
# CI_CLASSIFIER_MODEL=anthropic/claude-haiku-4.5      # $1.00/$5.00   DEFAULT — reliable JSON, one vendor, no surprises
# CI_CLASSIFIER_MODEL=z-ai/glm-5.3-flash              # $0.045/$0.14  ~30× cheaper than Haiku. The cost pick.
# CI_CLASSIFIER_MODEL=deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash    # $0.15/$0.60   middle ground, non-reasoning, fast
# CI_CLASSIFIER_MODEL=deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash      # $0.049/$0.097 cheapest credible; verify before trusting
# CI_CLASSIFIER_MODEL=qwen/qwen3-coder                # $0.30/$1.00   Alibaba Qwen
# CI_CLASSIFIER_MODEL=moonshotai/kimi-k2.5            # $0.60/$2.50   Kimi K2.5
# CI_CLASSIFIER_MODEL=deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro        # DO NOT — reasoning model. See trap 1.
# CI_CLASSIFIER_MODEL=anthropic/claude-opus-5         # DO NOT — thousands of calls; cost blows up.
# CI_CLASSIFIER_MODEL=z-ai/glm-4.5-air                # DO NOT — no response_format support; breaks classification.
#
# PROVE a swap, don't trust it. This writes nothing and shows which verdicts would CHANGE:
#   CI_CLASSIFIER_MODEL=z-ai/glm-5.3-flash npm run reclassify:dry
# Low, noisy disagreement → take the saving. Reclassifying `security_incident` as `noise`
# → don't. The classifier decides what you ever see, so its misses are invisible by design.
#
# Non-cost factor, your call: GLM is Zhipu AI and Qwen is Alibaba, both China-based, and
# the classifier sees every competitor signal you ingest. If that matters to your data
# governance it matters more than the money.

# ── Synthesis — battlecards, self-card, briefs. The artefacts humans actually read.
#    Cheapest is the WRONG objective here: degrading this to save a few dollars a month
#    damages the only output the product exists to produce.
# CI_SYNTHESIS_MODEL=anthropic/claude-sonnet-5        # $2.00/$10.00  DEFAULT — supersedes Sonnet 4.5 AND costs less
# CI_SYNTHESIS_MODEL=anthropic/claude-opus-5          # $5.00/$25.00  ~2.5× dearer; for flagship competitors only
# CI_SYNTHESIS_MODEL=anthropic/claude-haiku-4.5       # $1.00/$5.00   cheaper, but a triage model writing prose — expect it to read like one

# ── Deep reasoning — analyst /deep /gap /outside + `npm run research`.
#    Low volume, one call at a time, reasoning depth over everything.
# CI_DEEP_MODEL=anthropic/claude-opus-5               # $5.00/$25.00  DEFAULT — same price as the Opus 4.7 it replaced
# CI_DEEP_MODEL=anthropic/claude-sonnet-5             # $2.00/$10.00  ~60% cheaper; usually fine for /scan and /brief


# ─── OPTIONAL: notification tuning ───────────────────────────────────────────

# Only fire Windows toasts for signals with impactScore ≥ this value.
# Default 80 ("critical"). Set 0 = every signal; set 101 = disable toasts.
# CI_TOAST_THRESHOLD=80

# Hard cap on toasts per process run — prevents flooding on big batches.
# CI_TOAST_MAX_PER_RUN=5

# Dashboard URL used when a toast is clicked. Change if you serve elsewhere.
# CI_VIEWER_URL=http://localhost:5180


# ─── OPTIONAL: YouTube transcript ingestion ──────────────────────────────────

# When YouTube captions are unavailable, fall back to local Whisper.
# Requires `nodejs-whisper` model files — expect 1–5 GB disk use.
# CI_WHISPER_ENABLED=false
# CI_WHISPER_MODEL=base.en


# ─── CORPORATE-NETWORK WORKAROUND ────────────────────────────────────────────

# Set only if you're behind a TLS-intercepting proxy (Zscaler, Netskope, etc.)
# that breaks Node's cert verification.
#
# PREFER NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS. Pointing Node at the corporate root CA keeps verification
# ON; the flag below turns it OFF for every HTTPS call the process makes — including the
# ones carrying OPENROUTER_API_KEY and TURSO_AUTH_TOKEN.
# NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS=/path/to/corporate-root.pem
#
# THIS FLAG ROTS. It gets set once to get past a proxy and then outlives it silently,
# because nothing fails when it is no longer needed — `npm run doctor` warns for exactly
# that reason. Before keeping it, prove you still need it:
#
#   node -e "const t=require('tls');delete process.env.NODE_TLS_REJECT_UNAUTHORIZED;\
#   const s=t.connect({host:'openrouter.ai',port:443,servername:'openrouter.ai'},()=>{\
#   console.log('verified OK, issuer:',s.getPeerCertificate().issuer.O);s.end()});\
#   s.on('error',e=>console.log('still needed:',e.code))"
#
# "verified OK" with a public CA (Google Trust Services, Amazon, Sectigo…) means there is
# no interceptor and this line should be deleted.
# NODE_TLS_REJECT_UNAUTHORIZED=0

# ─── WHAT THIS DEPLOYMENT TRACKS AND WHAT AGENTS MAY DO ──────────────────────

# Every one of these is OPTIONAL. Each resolves in the same order:
#   $SIGNALS_*  →  config/<name>.local.mjs  →  config/<name>.default.mjs
# The .local.mjs files are gitignored, so customising any of them never
# conflicts with `git pull`. Set the variable only when you want a file that
# lives somewhere else entirely.

# The roster — which companies exist, and which one is the comparison anchor.
# SIGNALS_COMPANIES=config/companies.local.mjs

# Feeds. Derived from the roster, so most deployments never set this.
# SIGNALS_FEEDS=config/feeds.local.mjs

# Non-English news coverage. Every Google News feed defaults to hl=en-US&gl=US, so
# regional trade press is invisible — docs/blindspots.md carried that as a Medium gap.
# Each locale here adds ONE news feed per company plus one per category query, and every
# item they return is an item the classifier pays to judge. So it is opt-in: unset means
# en-US only, exactly as before. Format is lang:COUNTRY, comma separated.
# CI_NEWS_LOCALES=de-DE:DE,ja-JP:JP,fr-FR:FR

# Hard ceiling on LLM spend, in USD, over a rolling 24h window. Checked once per process
# before the first OpenRouter call, against the same llm_cost ledger the agent budget
# uses — cron, the CLI and every agent draw down one number.
#
# Unset = no ceiling (the shipped default). When the ceiling is hit the run STOPS rather
# than falling back to the keyword classifier: a silent downgrade writes low-quality
# rows into the permanent store, and that is not recoverable the way a halted run is.
# CI_LLM_DAILY_CEILING_USD=5.00

# Battle's relevance-ranking axes (shipped: Codebase / Team size).
# SIGNALS_DEAL_CONTEXT=config/deal-context.local.mjs

# What a new subdomain or sitemap path is worth. Read by cert-watch,
# sitemap-watch AND the dashboard, so all three agree.
# SIGNALS_SUBDOMAIN_SIGNALS=config/subdomain-signals.local.mjs

# What an MCP agent may trigger, and its rolling 24h spend ceiling. Ships
# READ-ONLY: no paid action is enabled until you list one here.
# SIGNALS_AGENT_POLICY=config/agent-policy.local.mjs

# Answer-engine prompts for `npm run watch:aeo`.
# SIGNALS_AEO_PROMPTS=config/aeo-prompts.local.mjs


# ─── OPTIONAL: Gmail Google Alerts (Path A′ — local Zone 1) ─────────────────
# Full design: docs/gmail.md  ·  Security SoT: docs/decisions/gmail-ingest.md
# Architecture: Gmail API gmail.readonly → local data/email/inbox.db → email:promote → signals.
# App passwords / IMAP are REJECTED. Do NOT put the refresh token on Railway in v1.
# MCP never talks to Gmail. Zone 1 never calls OpenRouter.
#
# 1) Dedicated Gmail + label (default Signal/Alerts) + filter from googlealerts-noreply
# 2) GCP project → Gmail API → Desktop OAuth client → consent screen In production
# 3) Fill the two client values, then: npm run gmail:oauth
# 4) npm run watch:gmail   then   npm run email:promote:nollm
#
# CI_GMAIL_CLIENT_ID=
# CI_GMAIL_CLIENT_SECRET=
# CI_GMAIL_LABEL=Signal/Alerts
# Fixed loopback for npm run gmail:oauth (must match Console redirect URI exactly):
# CI_GMAIL_OAUTH_PORT=8765
# → register: http://127.0.0.1:8765/oauth2/callback
#
# Caps (defaults shown):
# CI_GMAIL_MAX_MESSAGES_PER_RUN=40
# CI_GMAIL_MAX_HITS_PER_RUN=200     per message, per run; a capped message saves its
#                                   offset and the next run resumes from there
# CI_EMAIL_MAX_CLASSIFY_PER_RUN=50
# CI_EMAIL_MAX_SIGNALS_PER_DAY=150  measured across ALL runs since 00:00 UTC, from the
#                                   local store's own promoted rows
#
# How many times a message may parse to zero hits before the warning escalates from
# "will retry" to "this is a parser bug". The message is never marked seen either way:
# CI_GMAIL_ZERO_HIT_WARN_AFTER=3
#
# Local Zone 1 DB override. Must be a local path or a file: URL — any other scheme is
# refused outright, because this is the process that holds the Gmail token:
# CI_GMAIL_LOCAL_DB=file:./data/email/inbox.db
#
# Promote into hosted Turso only after deliberate review:
# CI_EMAIL_PROMOTE_ALLOW_PROD=1
#
# Dev-only env token (prefer CredMan/DPAPI via gmail:oauth):
# CI_GMAIL_REFRESH_TOKEN=
# CI_GMAIL_ALLOW_ENV_TOKEN=1

# Optional: where `npm run shot` writes dashboard captures. Default `.screenshots/`
# (gitignored). Point it at a notes vault or anywhere outside the repo if you keep
# captures there. Relative paths resolve from the repo root.
# SIGNALS_SCREENSHOTS_DIR=.screenshots
